## Required finding F01 — malformed-input promise is broader than the implementation

Location: source_preparation/verify_exact.py lines 35–45, especially the promise at line 37 and early return at lines 44–45; source_preparation/README.md line 37.

The function promises that malformed input raises, and the README says malformed literals are rejected. In both normal Python and Python -O, normalize([[], [0]]), normalize([[], [True]]) and normalize([[], [1, 2, 3, 4]]) instead return the fixed no-instance normalization without checking the later malformed clause. Reversing each clause order raises the intended literal or clause-size guard. The documented rejection behavior is therefore order dependent. NORMALIZATION_NORMAL.json, NORMALIZATION_OPTIMIZED.json and NORMALIZATION_EXECUTION_RECEIPTS.json preserve the exact six probes and actual process receipts.

This is a minor diagnostic-contract/documentation accuracy issue. It does not invalidate either reduction direction on valid at-most-three-literal formulas. An empty valid clause already makes every valid formula unsatisfiable. No cost-table, bound or mathematical error follows from these probes.

Required disposition: either validate all raw clauses before the empty-clause shortcut, or explicitly narrow the contract and README to disclose that suffix validation stops after an empty clause. The parent has selected full validation. Add the three suffix cases and their ordering controls to the published finite diagnostics under normal and optimized Python, then regenerate the affected receipts/manifests consistently. Do not overwrite V1 or present its prior check counts as V2 counts. No other required or optional findings were identified.

